R=rows('''
我们六个人每周四下午五点开会，一般聊四十五分钟。有时五点半客户打电话，一位同事就要出去接，回来又问刚才决定了什么。我想把会议改早、改短，出于方便大家的考虑，先提出周三三点、二十分钟。说出来时我觉得很简单，听到同事的问题，才发现自己只想到了会议里面的时间。|Our six-person team met Thursdays at five for about forty-five minutes. A customer call at 5:30 sometimes took a colleague out, who then needed the decisions repeated. I proposed Wednesday at three for twenty minutes. It sounded simple until colleagues' questions showed I’d counted only time inside the meeting.
第一个问题是准备材料。我建议每个人提前写三点，只讨论需要一起决定的事情。同事问，会开短了，材料会不会反而要做一整页。我说不用做一页，每点两三句话就好，后来也把准备时间放进记录里。短的目标不是把同样长的内容换个地方做，而是让大家先知道最需要决定的是什么。|I suggested three points in advance, discussing only joint decisions. A colleague worried preparation would become a full-page document. I proposed two or three sentences per point and included preparation time in the record. The goal wasn’t moving equally long work elsewhere, but identifying the most important decisions.
第二个问题是时间。我只问了三个人，觉得三点大概方便，就先写进方案。另外一位说，周三三点要见客户，不能来。我这才把还没收到的答复和已经同意分开。我们换成周四三点这个选择，但没有立刻发通知。我到周二下班前问完六个人，收到答复以后才确定两周试用。|I had checked only three people’s availability and assumed three was likely suitable. Another had a customer appointment on Wednesday at three. I separated missing replies from agreement. We considered Thursday at three, but waited until all six had replied before Tuesday’s finish to confirm the two-week trial.
第一次按新安排开会，我准备三点用了五分钟，会议用了十八分钟。有人没有问题要一起决定，就简单说这周按原安排继续。大家结束时知道谁负责联系场地、谁问人数、谁确认客户的时间。我觉得轻松了一些，不过没有说十八分钟本身就能证明有成效；如果结束以后还得四处问刚才决定了什么，短也没有解决问题。|In the first trial I spent five minutes preparing and eighteen in the meeting. People without joint questions briefly confirmed existing work. At the end responsibilities for venue, numbers and customer time were clear. I felt relief, without treating eighteen minutes alone as proof of effectiveness; having to chase decisions afterwards would still be a problem.
第二次有一个场地问题。原来能用的房间临时要维修，活动是否改到室内，和采购什么东西都有关系。我们用了二十六分钟，没有在二十分钟一到就假装问题已经讲完。最后两个负责的人又谈了十分钟，我也是其中一个。那天我准备材料用了十二分钟，把自己的时间加起来，一共四十八分钟，比第一次多得很明显。|The second trial involved room repairs affecting the indoor venue and purchases. We took twenty-six minutes rather than pretending the issue was finished at twenty. Two responsible people, including me, then talked for ten minutes. With my twelve-minute preparation, my total was forty-eight minutes, noticeably more than the first trial.
同事提醒，四十八分钟不能直接和以前四十五分钟相比，因为以前我们只记过会议时间，没有记我准备了多久。他说得对。我们能看出这次有额外工作，也能发现哪部分需要改，但还不能说整个方案一定省时间。把没记录的时间当成零，结果就会看起来比实际好；拿一次有问题的会当作所有会议，也会太快下结论。|A colleague noted that forty-eight couldn’t directly be compared with the old forty-five: we’d recorded only the old meeting, not my preparation. We could identify extra work and revisions, but not claim the whole plan saved time. Treating unrecorded time as zero would flatter the result, while one problematic meeting couldn’t represent them all.
两周以后，我们先保留周四三点，再调整材料。每个人除了三点，也写一句最需要谁回答；只有两个人相关的细节，能提前问就提前问。采购的人要知道实际数量，不用等全组都看完颜色和包装。我们没有减少需要一起决定的问题，只把不需要六个人一起等的内容分开，另外花的时间仍然记录。|After two weeks we kept Thursday at three and added who needed to answer each question. Details involving only two people could be checked earlier. Purchasers needed actual quantities without waiting for everyone to inspect colours and packaging. We separated work not requiring all six while still recording the additional time.
这个方案从最初一个简单想法，变成了有条件的尝试。有人愿意试，有人先问代价，都帮我看见没想到的地方。我们没有因为第二次用了二十六分钟，就把所有内容恢复到原样，也没有因为第一次只有十八分钟，就说以后只能这样。接下来再记录两周，把问题和改法一起留下，才知道哪些部分值得继续。|The simple idea became a conditional trial. Willingness and objections both exposed gaps. We didn’t restore everything after the twenty-six-minute meeting or make the eighteen-minute one a permanent rule. Two more weeks of recording problems and revisions would show which parts deserved keeping.
''')
P=rows('''
同事提出把会改到周三三点，我先说这个时间不行。我那天下午要见客户，时间已经约好，不想因为内部会议再改。我担心他把这个回答理解成我反对所有变化，就补了一句：改短可以试，但要先问完大家。我们讨论的不是谁更愿意合作，而是哪个安排真的能让六个人到场。|When the colleague proposed Wednesday at three, I objected because of a confirmed customer appointment. I clarified I wasn’t rejecting every change: shorter meetings were worth trying after checking everyone’s availability. The issue wasn’t who was more cooperative, but what actually let all six attend.
他说只收到三个人答复，我问另外三个人怎么办。没答复的人可能还没看见，也可能要确认自己的安排，不能算作同意。后来他问了周四三点，到周二下班前收齐回复，才发通知。我看见这个过程，比较放心，因为新时间不是一边说要确认，一边已经替所有人答应。|Only three replies had arrived. Others might not have seen the request or might need to check their calendars, so silence wasn’t agreement. He checked Thursday at three and sent notice only after receiving all replies before Tuesday’s finish. That process reassured me; confirmation wasn’t promised after deciding on everyone’s behalf.
我还问了材料的长度。以前我参加过一种很短的会，每个人会前要做很长的介绍，结果会议时间少了，准备时间更多。这个同事听完，没有说我想得太多，而是把记录增加到包括准备。他也同意每点只写两三句，先说明问题，不需要把所有背景都写成一个新的报告。|I’d attended short meetings requiring long advance presentations, cutting meeting time but increasing preparation. He listened and included preparation in the record. Each point would be two or three sentences identifying a problem, rather than making all background into a new report.
第一次十八分钟结束，我知道自己下午要确认哪几个客户的时间，没有再去问一次。这个结果对我比十八这个数字更重要。另一个同事负责场地，结束前发现自己漏听了人数，就当场问清。我们没有为了守住二十分钟，把他的问题留到一封又一封邮件里，反而趁大家在一起补了一句。|After the first eighteen-minute meeting I knew which customer times to confirm. That mattered more than the number eighteen. The venue organiser clarified a missed attendance number before finishing. We didn’t protect twenty minutes by sending the question into repeated later emails; we settled it together.
第二次场地要维修，采购和活动安排都受到影响。会开了二十六分钟，后来还有两个人继续谈。我不是那两个人，就先去做自己的工作。有人说这说明方案没有用，我觉得还要看谁花了什么时间、事情有没有办好。没参加后面的讨论，不代表那十分钟不存在；但也不能把十分钟再算成每个人都等了十分钟。|Room repairs made the second meeting twenty-six minutes, followed by two people talking separately. I wasn’t involved and resumed work. To judge usefulness we needed to know who spent which time and whether the work was done. The ten minutes existed, but couldn’t be counted as all six waiting ten more minutes.
准备材料最多的同事说自己那天一共用了四十八分钟。我提醒他，以前的准备没有记录，四十五只是会议本身。我们不是想让数字永远好看，也不是一看到增加就停止。知道旧记录少了什么，才能公平一点比较。后来我们把记录写得更具体，会议、个人准备、另外讨论分别记，不把所有时间混在同一个格子里。|The colleague with the most preparation reported forty-eight total minutes. I reminded him the old forty-five was only the meeting. We weren’t trying to flatter numbers or stop at any increase. Understanding the missing baseline allowed fairer comparison. We recorded meetings, personal preparation and separate discussion in different fields.
两周的尝试结束，我同意继续周四三点，但提出新的问题：如果客户临时需要联系，有没有办法先把我负责的内容说完。我不要求每次都排第一，只希望有变化时能协调。对方说先问当天有没有急的事情，再决定顺序。我觉得这样比一年固定一种顺序更符合我们的工作，也还需要在后面试。|After two weeks I supported keeping Thursday at three and asked about covering my items early if a customer unexpectedly needed me. I wasn’t demanding always going first. We would check urgent matters and set an order each day. That seemed better suited to our work than a year-long fixed sequence, though still needing trials.
我对这个方案的评价，不是同意的人多就一定好，也不是有人提反对就一定不行。最有用的变化是大家能说明原因，再把实际办法改出来。提出想法的人没有失去面子，提问题的人也没有被叫作不合作。我们还会有分歧，但至少知道下次该拿什么情况来谈，而不是只说我觉得快、你觉得慢。|My judgment wasn’t that many supporters proved it good or one objection proved it bad. The useful change was explaining reasons and revising actions. Neither proposal nor objection meant losing face or being called uncooperative. Disagreement would remain, but now we knew what evidence to discuss beyond feelings of fast or slow.
''')
G=[
card('拿…来说','Introduce a specific example','拿 plus a topic and 来说 draws attention to an example, often followed by what it shows. One example does not automatically represent all cases.','拿第二次会议来说，准备时间也增加了。|Take the second meeting: preparation time increased too.\n拿周三来说，我三点要见客户。|Take Wednesday: I have a customer meeting at three.','拿第二次来说，所以每次都一样。','拿第二次会议来说，准备时间也增加了。','The example supports that meeting; it does not prove every meeting identical.'),
card('以便…','Give the intended enabling purpose','以便 introduces what an action should make easier or possible. It is common in written or formal workplace explanations; the purpose is not a guaranteed result.','请提前写出问题，以便大家准备。|Please write the questions ahead so everyone can prepare.\n先确认人数，以便采购。|Confirm numbers first to make purchasing possible.','请提前写出问题，以便大家已经准备完了。','请提前写出问题，以便大家准备。','The purpose points forward rather than asserting preparation is already complete.'),
card('尽管…，但是…','Concede a real circumstance','尽管 introduces an accepted fact; 但是 gives a point that remains despite it. Compare a hypothetical 即使 clause.','尽管会议短了，但是准备可能更长。|Although the meeting is shorter, preparation may be longer.\n尽管只试了两次，但是我们已经发现一个问题。|Although we tried it only twice, we have found a problem.','尽管还没试，但是已经知道全部结果。','尽管只试了两次，但是我们已经发现一个问题。','State the actually observed concession and limit the conclusion to the known problem.'),
card('一旦…，就…','Plan a response to a possible trigger','一旦 marks the condition becoming true; 就 introduces the response. It often sounds more formal than 要是 and does not assert the trigger already occurred.','一旦时间有变动，就重新确认。|Once the time changes, confirm again.\n一旦人数定下来，就可以采购。|Once numbers are settled, purchasing can begin.','一旦人数定下来，所以人数已经定了。','一旦人数定下来，就可以采购。','A planned conditional action does not establish that numbers are settled now.'),
card('V下来 / V起来','Distinguish settling a result and beginning a process','定下来 points to reaching a settled result. 讨论起来 marks beginning discussion. Context matters; do not replace all direction complements interchangeably.','时间定下来以后再发通知。|Send notice after the time is settled.\n大家讨论起来，才发现还有问题。|Only as discussion began did everyone discover more issues.','大家讨论下来才开始，时间定起来就结束。','大家讨论起来，时间以后再定下来。','Use 起来 for starting discussion and 下来 for a settled time in these frames.'),
]
